1: # tests/norm_checks/
2: 
3: One subdirectory per round, `round_{N}/`, with two files from two
4: different authors:
5: 
6: - `norm_plan.json` — written by `norm-architect` (2026-09-24: semantic
7:   compilation only). Every atomic requirement the round's norm implies,
8:   classified as ROLE/ACTION/OBJECT/RULE/VISIBILITY/LIFECYCLE, each with an
9:   `agent_experience` block (what a fisher now knows, decides, may/may not
10:   do, remembers, observes). norm-architect has no tools and no repository
11:   access beyond one conceptual doc; it cannot write a file path, an
12:   import, or a test scenario, so it doesn't try to.
13: - `test_round_{N}.py` — written by `norm-engineer`, which decides what
14:   each requirement needs tested (grounded in that requirement's own
15:   `agent_experience` block) and writes a real pytest function named
16:   `test_{requirement_id}_{scenario}` for it, before writing any
17:   implementation code. `norm-engineer` reads `norm_plan.json` before
18:   writing any code (it's the concrete target it must satisfy) but cannot
19:   edit it — permission-denied by design, so the agent being judged against
20:   a plan can never adjust the plan to fit whatever it happens to build.
21: 
22: Write however many tests a requirement actually needs — never just one.
23: At minimum the compliant path, the non-compliant/penalty path where the
24: requirement implies one, and the boundary cases the norm's own numbers
25: imply (exactly at a threshold, just under it, just over it).
26: 
27: 2026-09-24 (same day, second change): norm-architect originally also
28: proposed acceptance-test SPECIFICATIONS (given/when/expect) here, with the
29: Harness Validator (`validate_norm_plan()`) requiring at least one per
30: ROLE/ACTION/RULE/VISIBILITY requirement before norm-engineer's session
31: ever started. Dropped after a real run showed this discarding whole
32: rounds outright — DeepSeek-R1 didn't reliably converge on covering every
33: flagged gap even across the validator's one bounded retry pass (see round
34: 1 and round 2 of `sim/run-20260924-005713`: both discarded here, before
35: norm-engineer ever ran). norm-engineer now owns deciding scenarios
36: entirely, and the validator no longer checks for their presence.
37: 
38: Distinct from `tests/regression/`: that directory is a fixed, human-owned
39: suite neither `norm-architect` nor `norm-engineer` may ever edit or
40: weaken. This directory is the opposite — freshly authored every round,
41: and expected to start out failing red (nothing implementing the round's
42: norm exists yet) until `norm-engineer` makes it pass.
43: 
44: A requirement's tests passing is necessary, not sufficient, for the round
45: to be judged compliant. After a clean round, the harness assembles a
46: structured evidence package (`_gather_norm_evidence()`,
47: `state/norm_evidence/round_N.json`) — per requirement,
48: `norm-finalizer`'s independently verified claims plus each test's own
49: PASS/FAIL. `norm-auditor` (a plain, tool-free completion call, not a
50: separate test-writing agent) then reviews norm.txt against
51: `norm_plan.json` and that evidence package together, specifically to
52: catch under-enforcement a passing test couldn't have caught on its own (a
53: test that's technically green but itself checks something weaker than the
54: norm's own text demands) — this is now the *only* backstop against a
55: self-authored test that's too weak, since norm-engineer both picks the
56: scenarios and writes the assertions.
57: 
58: A purely parametric round (writing only to `state/config.json`/
59: `state/fluents.json`) doesn't need a new test here — there's no new code
60: to cover. The orchestrator's Self-Correction Gate
61: (`norm_implementation_failing_tests_errors()` in `engine/simulate.py`)
62: runs `pytest tests/norm_checks/round_{N}/` automatically every repair
63: attempt, alongside `tests/regression/`, feeding any failure's stack trace
64: straight back to `norm-engineer` — this is the mechanical part of the TDD
